most-probable-state-68ca9993.interpretation:
This equation describes the ratio of the number of particles or systems in state 1 (
        n_1
      ) to the number of particles in state 2 (
        n_2
      ). The ratio is directly proportional to the ratio of their degeneracies (
        g_1 / g_2
      ), which accounts for the number of distinct quantum states that have the same energy. The term 
        e^{-(E_1 - E_2)/k_B T}
      is the Boltzmann factor, which shows that the relative population of the states depends exponentially on the energy difference (
        E_1 - E_2
      ) and the absolute temperature (
        T
      ). A larger energy difference (
        E_1 > E_2
      ) or a lower temperature (
        T
      ) leads to a smaller ratio, indicating that state 2 (the lower energy state) is more populated. Conversely, at high temperatures or with small energy differences, the populations tend to equalize.

